\DOC X86_GEN_ACCSTEPS_TAC

\TYPE {X86_GEN_ACCSTEPS_TAC : (string -> tactic) -> thm * (thm option array) -> int list -> int list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes a block of x86 instructions in an {eventually} goal,
advancing the goal's state to a later state, replacing the old state's
register/memory/flag assumptions with ones about the new state, and adding
accumulated arithmetic (carry/product) equations as assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_GEN_ACCSTEPS_TAC acc_preproc th anums snums} takes:
{acc_preproc : string -> tactic}, a tactic generator applied during the
stepping (given a state name) to post-process assumptions, e.g. rewriting them
with word identities — a constant function returning {ALL_TAC} does nothing
extra; {th : thm * (thm option array)}, an execution record for the program,
namely a theorem giving the length of the code together with a cached array of
decoded-instruction theorems; {anums : int list}, the instruction numbers whose
effects are summarised as accumulated arithmetic equations; and
{snums : int list}, the instruction numbers to step through. Writing {S(s)} for
the state-indexed assumptions and {Acc} for the accumulated equations:
{
       A, S(s_i) ?- eventually ... s_i
       ==============================================  X86_GEN_ACCSTEPS_TAC acc_preproc th anums snums
       A, Acc, S(s_j) ?- eventually ... s_j
}
\noindent The deleted assumptions {S(s_i)} are those describing the previous
state: code loadedness, program-counter, register, memory and flag reads, and
the frame/{MAYCHANGE} relation for that state. The added assumptions {S(s_j)}
are, for the new state: the code-loadedness fact, reads of registers, flags and
memory locations, an updated {MAYCHANGE} composition relating the original and
new state, and the new program-counter value. The accumulated equations {Acc}
are signed-arithmetic equations of the form {2 pow 64 * <high/carry> + <low> =
<expression>}, recording the multiply/add/subtract results of the stepped
instructions. Purely arithmetic side assumptions that do not mention a state
may be re-added unchanged, so their positions shift.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-goal state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about an x86
machine state.

\ENDDOC
